WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.339
I'm going to use some physics to answer the question of whether or not Rapunzel really could

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:08.039
support someone's weight just with her hair.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.460
So I'm going to make a few assumptions.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:15.300
The first is that it's possible to grow your hair to be 20 meters long,

00:00:15.309 --> 00:00:18.989
and that's a conservative estimate of what Rapunzel's length was.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.660
Hair usually takes

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.780
one year to grow about 15 cm (5.9 inches)

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:30.540
So, to grow 20 meters of hair would usually take 133 years

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.380
That's if everything else goes well.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.800
The current world record is about 5.5 m (18 ft),

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.440
And mine, probably a measly

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.780
1.3 meters (4.2 ft)? Something like that.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.180
But, let's assume it's possible.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.200
Now, Rapunzel's hair is going to weigh a lot

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.500
I found one estimate that twelve centimeters (4.7 inches) of hair would weigh

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.120
0.62 milligrams.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:00.620
That works out to be 103 mg for 20m (65.5 ft) of hair.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:06.620
Multiply that by 150000 hairs on an average blonde head,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.900
and we end up with 15.5 kilograms (3.3 lbs) of hair.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:13.740
Now let's look at some forces

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.660
Downwards, we have the force of gravity acting on the hair

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.360
that's going to be 155 Newtons.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.480
Add the weight of say, a prince, and

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.240
take his mass to be 80 kilograms (176 lbs).

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.640
That gives us a total downwards force of

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.740
955 Newtons.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.520
Now, there's actually no way that Rapunzel's neck would be able to withstand

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.380
that kind of force.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:46.700
And in fact, one of the weakest components in this is going to the the bonds between

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.040
her hair and her scalp and connecting to the skull.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:59.040
So... the hair would actually probably get ripped out there if all of that force is going straight to the roots.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:03.100
However, that doesn't mean it's not possible to hold that weight.

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:07.559
Rapunzel just needs to hook her hair or tie it around something

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.260
to take that force directly off her neck and head.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.060
If she can do that, then lifting a prince

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.260
shouldn't actually be a problem, because in fact,

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:21.680
human hair is incredibly strong.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.380
Each strand of it can hold about 100 grams.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.640
That's about one apple.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.580
So, put together, for a hundred and fifty thousand strands,

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.700
human hair should be able to lift

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.360
15000 kgs (33069.3 lbs).

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:40.840
That's incredible and that's a casual 188 princes.

